-------------------------------------------------------------------------
Interpretation
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
* memcpy single-core is NOT stable on this host: three samples on core 4
  gave 6787.2 / 8574.5 / 9991.8 MB/s (spread 47 %, median 8574.5).
  The host runs at load avg ~4.2 with ffmpeg/mihomo/frpc/rclone active,
  and memory churn (used 4006 -> 4396 MiB, available 3908 -> 3518 MiB
  across the session; each 256 MiB buffer set is 768 MiB of that).
  Treat ~8.5-10 GB/s as the A76 memcpy ceiling and 6.8 GB/s as a
  loaded-host floor. Do not read 26-47 % differences as real.
* XOR is ~half of memcpy throughput (3702-4372 MB/s single core), i.e.
  the 2-stream read + 1-stream write pattern costs about what a third
  memory stream predicts.
* Thread scaling on the four A76 cores is poor and memory-bound:
  memcpy 8574.5 -> 8795.8 MB/s (x1.03), XOR 4372.3 -> 4851.8 (x1.11).
  Adding cores buys almost nothing for these kernels; the RK3588
  memory controller, not the cores, is the limit.
* RLNC encode scales as expected with d, and not at all with W:
  d=8 ~2.5 GB/s, d=12 ~1.67 GB/s, d=16 ~1.26 GB/s (0.39 / 0.59 / 0.78
  ns per byte). W=8/16/32 land within ~5 % of each other, so the
  generation width costs nothing per byte -- consistent with the code
  XOR-ing d sources per parity packet regardless of W.
* RLNC decode (Gauss-Jordan, W=16) sits at 1.5-1.75 GB/s for k=2..8
